# New task workflow

When the user asks to **start a new task**, **pick the next task**, or **continue with new work**, run these steps **in order** before writing code.

## 1. Sync production

```powershell
& "C:\Program Files\Git\cmd\git.exe" checkout 1-production
& "C:\Program Files\Git\cmd\git.exe" pull origin 1-production
```

Confirm local `1-production` matches `origin/1-production`.

## 2. Create Jira ticket → In Progress

- **Site:** `https://bridos1234.atlassian.net`
- **Project:** `KAN`
- **Issue type:** Task (unless the user specifies Bug/Story)

Use Atlassian MCP:

1. `createJiraIssue` — `cloudId`: `https://bridos1234.atlassian.net`, `projectKey`: `KAN`, clear `summary` and short `description` of the work
2. `getTransitionsForJiraIssue` on the new key — find the transition to **In Progress**
3. `transitionJiraIssue` — apply that transition

If the task scope is unclear, propose summary/description and confirm with the user before creating the ticket.

## 3. Create branch

Branch off updated `1-production`. Naming: see `branch-naming.mdc`.

```powershell
& "C:\Program Files\Git\cmd\git.exe" checkout -b KAN-{n}-{short-description}
```

Use the **ticket number from step 2** in the branch name. Short description: lowercase, hyphen-separated, 2–4 words.

## After the workflow

Tell the user the Jira key, branch name, and what you are building next. Then proceed with TDD unless they say otherwise.

## End-of-task reminder (not part of “new task”)

When work is complete, follow these steps **in order**:

1. Tests green
2. Commit (`feat:`/`fix:`)
3. Push branch
4. Open PR against `1-production`
5. Move Jira ticket → **In Review** (use `transitionJiraIssue`)
6. Wait — **user reviews and merges the PR**, then notifies you
7. Move Jira ticket → **Done** (use `transitionJiraIssue`)
8. Pull `1-production` locally
